    {
      "id": "M4208-A",
      "skill": "engineering-journal-rigor-review-zh",
      "language": "zh",
      "prompt": "A student reports two training-seed runs evaluated on the SAME 80 singly labeled test items. Both classes are included. Confusion-matrix rows are true [fault,normal] and columns predicted [fault,normal]. Run 1 is [[18,2],[42,18]], run 2 [[6,14],[2,58]]. All table entries are arithmetic means of the per-run scores; support weights are the true test-class frequencies. Reported percentages are: fault Precision 52.50, Recall 60.00, F1 43.93; normal F1 66.44; weighted F1 61.70; weighted Recall 62.50; Accuracy 63.50. A reviewer wants to replace fault F1 by the harmonic mean of the displayed Precision/Recall. A figure explicitly shows run 1 as a prespecified representative run; another appendix gives the sum of the two matrices and labels it a pooled prediction-count summary.\n\nAudit the actual arithmetic and classify apparent versus real inconsistencies. Explain what can be checked from class-wise means under fixed supports. State the appropriate status for the representative and pooled figures, and whether the repeated predictions imply 160 independently acquired test items. No authorization is given to change results. Give precise verification/correction requests for the author, not an instruction to rerun everything or automatically overwrite numbers. Write a short English author-facing summary of about 45-70 whitespace-delimited words that states the supported review finding without a defensive checklist.",
      "required_behaviors": [
        "Check per-run, class-weighted and pooled arithmetic from the supplied counts with the stated class order.",
        "Distinguish genuine inconsistent cells from valid nonlinear/representative-run differences without overwriting results."
      ],
      "forbidden_behaviors": [
        "Replace mean F1 by F1 of mean precision/recall or treat all run-averaged consistency checks as impossible.",
        "Certify the reported incorrect summary cells, count repeated predictions as new acquisitions or automatically alter results."
      ],
      "tested_skills": [
        "engineering-journal-rigor-review-zh"
      ],
      "outcome": "pass",
      "reason": "Distinguished nonlinear per-run summaries from valid fixed-support consistency checks and real table errors without changing results.",
      "evaluator": "Maintainer semantic review; no independent second grader",
      "response_trace": "responses/v4208-independent.md",
      "review_trace": "reviews/v4208-independent-evaluation.md",
      "evaluation_phase": "one_fresh_context_on_frozen_aggregation_records"
    },
    {
      "id": "M4208-B",
      "skill": "engineering-journal-rigor-review-zh",
      "language": "zh",
      "prompt": "Now consider two different evaluation folds, still of size 80, with matrices [[18,2],[42,18]] and [[54,6],[2,18]] in the same order. A report gives mean class-wise F1 of 69.05% for fault and 63.41% for normal, and mean support-weighted F1 of 67.64%. A reviewer pools the class supports from both folds into equal weights and proposes replacing the last figure by the average of the two displayed class-F1 means. Determine what this comparison actually establishes using the supplied run-level counts, without imposing fixed supports that are not present.\n\nSeparately, a fault-only recall table for Request A excludes the normal class and reports 60.00%; its overall Accuracy is 62.50%. In a two-label multilabel fixture, y_true=[[1,1],[1,0]] and y_pred=[[1,0],[1,0]]. Compute its support-weighted label recall and exact-set accuracy. Explain which cross-metric equality checks, if any, apply to these different scoring definitions. End with a concise Chinese handoff separating confirmed arithmetic issues, valid aggregation differences and missing metadata; do not introduce new methods, experimental benefits or general-purpose name admissions.",
      "required_behaviors": [
        "Use run-level support weights when they vary and preserve valid selected-class/multilabel conventions.",
        "Separate confirmed arithmetic, legitimate aggregation differences and missing metadata without inventing new acquisitions or experiments."
      ],
      "forbidden_behaviors": [
        "Apply fixed-support commutation when fold supports vary or equate multilabel subset accuracy with support-weighted recall.",
        "Require all figures to match mean-run metrics or invent parameter changes, reruns or outcome benefits."
      ],
      "tested_skills": [
        "engineering-journal-rigor-review-zh"
      ],
      "outcome": "pass",
      "reason": "Used actual varying supports and preserved selected-class and multilabel metric boundaries, with a scoped author handoff.",
      "evaluator": "Maintainer semantic review; no independent second grader",
      "response_trace": "responses/v4208-independent.md",
